# Each layer's remainder, with its fit worked out and its exposure measured. One row per layer that
# states both a demand and a nameplate and has a remainder this model can stand behind. The key is
# (filing, layer), and every question about a layer's fit reads this relation.
#
# Two ways to work out a remainder
#   On a layer a parent composes from parts, the remainder is each part's own remainder, converted
#   once into the parent's unit, less what the parent removed from its demand and its nameplate.
#   Everywhere else it is the nameplate less the demand, from the two filed totals, each end of the
#   one set against the opposite end of the other. The two agree on every layer whose conversion
#   factors are single values. Where a factor is a range, the totals take that range in twice, and
#   the second way comes out wider.
#
# The wider figure only bounds the remainder
#   On those layers the totals give a bound, not the remainder. Read as the remainder, it would make
#   `derived_fit` read `transition` on a filing that is rightly `clearance`, and
#   checks/fit_disagrees, which compares the filed sign with `derived_fit`, would report a document
#   that is right.
#
# Where the parts' remainder is suspended, there is no row
#   On a composed layer the remainder from the parts is the only figure this relation stands behind.
#   Where it is suspended, the row is dropped rather than filled from the totals, and
#   composition/figureless_remainders.sqlc names those layers. `asrt:Part` says a converted
#   remainder is never worked out again from the totals, and a figure from the totals there would
#   accuse a correct stated quantity in every rule below. arithmetic/remainder.sqlc gives the
#   dropped layer its row and its reason, and algebra/remainder_in_force.sqlc counts the drop.
#
# Everything is read from the remainder in force
#   The inner query picks the remainder in force, and the outer one works out the fit, the exposure
#   and the magnitude from it, so none of the three can read a different remainder from the others.
#
# `derived_fit` compares two ranges
#   It compares the supply's range with the demand's, as ISO 286 compares a fit. `clearance`: supply
#   exceeds demand across the whole range. `interference`: demand exceeds supply across the whole
#   range. `transition`: the ranges overlap, so which one wins depends on where things land. A
#   yes-or-no reading cannot name the third case, and the corpus files it.
#
# Which arm comes first
#   The three cases never overlap except where a single nameplate value equals a single demand
#   value. There `clearance` and `interference` both hold, and the remainder is zero at every point.
#   The first arm wins and returns `clearance`. ISO 286 counts a fit with zero minimum clearance as
#   a clearance fit, and this model carries that answer over to the case. It is also the reading
#   whose consequence is right: nothing went unmet, so checks/clearance_with_unserved rules out the
#   two holders that cannot be there. Swapping the two arms would reclassify that case, and `Fit`'s
#   annotation states the order for that reason.
#
# What `exposure` is
#   What the filing's own numbers say could have gone unserved: the largest demand against the
#   smallest supply, never below zero. Nobody measured it. It is the most the two filed ranges
#   allow, so the rule built on it compares it with what the filing says went unserved.
#
# The magnitude
#   `m_low`, `m_mode` and `m_high` are the size of the remainder whichever way it points, as three
#   values, worked out once here so that checks/shares_do_not_sum and
#   checks/stated_quantity_is_not_the_magnitude read the same figure. Where the remainder runs from
#   short to spare, its smallest size is zero.
-- layers/differenced_remainder.sqlc, overridden by composition/fused_remainders.sqlc where a
-- composed layer owes an exact remainder, less composition/figureless_remainders.sqlc.
SELECT x.filing, x.layer,
       x.sign, x.sign_absent,
       x.absorber_taxonomy, x.absorber_value, x.absorber_absent,
       x.d_low, x.d_mode, x.d_high, x.unit,
       x.n_low, x.n_mode, x.n_high, x.amount_unit,
       x.r_low, x.r_mode, x.r_high,
       CASE WHEN x.r_low  >= 0 THEN 'clearance'::pm.fit
            WHEN x.r_high <= 0 THEN 'interference'::pm.fit
            ELSE 'transition'::pm.fit END AS derived_fit,
       greatest(-x.r_low, 0) AS exposure,
       x.lumpy, x.quantum_mode, x.quantum_unit,
       x.pivoted,
       CASE WHEN x.r_low <= 0 AND x.r_high >= 0 THEN 0
            ELSE least(abs(x.r_low), abs(x.r_high)) END AS m_low,
       abs(x.r_mode)                                    AS m_mode,
       greatest(abs(x.r_low), abs(x.r_high))            AS m_high
FROM (
    SELECT b.filing, b.layer,
           b.sign, b.sign_absent,
           b.absorber_taxonomy, b.absorber_value, b.absorber_absent,
           b.d_low, b.d_mode, b.d_high, b.unit,
           b.n_low, b.n_mode, b.n_high, b.amount_unit,
           coalesce(f.pivoted_low,  b.r_low)  AS r_low,
           coalesce(f.pivoted_mode, b.r_mode) AS r_mode,
           coalesce(f.pivoted_high, b.r_high) AS r_high,
           b.lumpy, b.quantum_mode, b.quantum_unit,
           (f.pivoted_low IS NOT NULL) AS pivoted
    FROM      (
        :compose(layers/differenced_remainder.sqlc)
    ) b
    LEFT JOIN (
        :compose(composition/fused_remainders.sqlc)
    ) f ON f.composition = b.filing AND f.composed_layer = b.layer
    WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM ( :compose(composition/figureless_remainders.sqlc) ) g
                      WHERE g.filing = b.filing AND g.layer = b.layer)
) x
